From 391c9bfa0ac6e9db9168f96390a45f05b34f7e7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Filyus <filyus@filyus.ru>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:35:57 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add tokenHelper, compatible with pnpm

A per-registry `tokenHelper` names an absolute path to an executable that
prints a credential. It runs lazily, at most once per registry per process
(a failure is cached too), with an empty stdin, and never for an offline
request. Its output is trimmed, a leading byte order mark included; a raw
token is sent as Bearer, and output that starts with an auth scheme is used
as the header value, as in pnpm. It takes precedence over other credentials
for the same registry key.

The helper has 60 seconds: at the deadline the request fails and the helper
gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL; on Windows the whole process tree is ended with
taskkill, since the real helper behind a .cmd is a grandchild of cmd.exe.
More than 1 MiB of output also ends it. Every failure, including a spawn
that throws, rejects with ETOKENHELPER and a cause of only { code, signal }.
stderr is logged as warnings with the helper's own credential, as it is
and as JSON, a shell's quotes and a URL write it, and anything that looks like
one masked. An error response to a request that carried the credential
has it hidden in the same forms from its message, body and headers. A body failure or an abort while the helper
runs rejects the request without waiting for it. On Windows a .cmd/.bat
helper runs in its own directory and a path containing `"` or `%` is
refused.

The credential is sent only over https:, or over http: to localhost, 127.0.0.1
or [::1]; a request over http: to the registry's host, such as a lockfile's
resolved URL, goes out without it and does not start the helper. A redirect
that keeps the host name keeps the credential only while the target may carry
it, and is followed here, a hop at a time, for that reason. A request that
carries it takes proxy, https-proxy, noproxy, ca, strict-ssl and local-address
from opts.tokenHelperTransport when the caller supplies one, instead of its
own, since only the caller knows which config file a value came from.

Prototype for the credential provider RFC discussed in npm/rfcs#850.
