 }
 ```
 
+##### <a name="opts-tokenhelpers"></a> `opts.tokenHelpers`
+
+* Type: Object, registry key (`//<registry>/`) to absolute path
+* Default: null
+
+For each registry key, an executable that prints a registry credential to stdout, mapped to a header
+as pnpm's `tokenHelper` does. Unlike pnpm, which splits the value on
+whitespace into a command and arguments, the value is one absolute path,
+which may contain spaces. It runs at most once per registry per process, the first
+time a request needs it, with no arguments and an empty stdin. The outcome is
+cached whether it succeeded or failed: after a failure, later requests for
+that registry fail with the same error without running the helper again. A raw
+token is sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; output that already starts
+with an auth scheme (`Bearer …`, `Basic …`) is used as the header value.
+Surrounding whitespace and a leading byte order mark are removed first. When
+set, it takes precedence over other credentials for the same registry key.
+
+The helper has 60 seconds. The request then fails at once, and the helper is
+sent SIGTERM and, if it is still running 2 seconds later, SIGKILL (on Windows
+the whole process tree is ended; elsewhere only the helper process itself, so
+a helper script should `exec` the command that does the work). Every other failure also rejects with code
+`ETOKENHELPER`: a non-zero exit, a signal, more than 1 MiB of output (which
+also ends the helper, as the deadline does), empty output, output that is not
+a valid header value (more than one line, a control or non-ASCII character;
+the message does not quote it), a path that is not absolute or has arguments, or an executable that
+cannot be started (a script whose `#!` interpreter is missing, or whose `#!`
+line ends in a carriage return, is reported as such). The error's `cause` carries only an exit code and signal,
+never the helper's output. Lines the helper writes to stderr are logged as
+`token-helper` warnings, with the helper's own credential and anything that
+looks like a credential (`Bearer …`, `Basic …`, `token=…`, `"password": "…"`)
+masked. If the registry answers a request that carried the credential with an
+error, the credential is hidden in the same forms (as is, JSON-escaped,
+shell-quoted, percent-encoded) from the error's `message`, `body` and `headers`,
+so a registry that echoes the `Authorization` header does not get it printed. A request whose `opts.signal` aborts, or whose body fails, while the
+helper runs is rejected without waiting for it; the helper itself keeps
+running, and its result is still cached for the requests that follow. Requests with `opts.offline`
+make no network request and do not run the helper.
+
+The helper starts in its own directory, not in the current one, and gets the
+process environment without the variables npm sets from its config
+(`NODE_OPTIONS`, `npm_config_*`, `EDITOR`, `NODE_ENV`, `COLOR`, `INIT_CWD`,
+`NODE`, `npm_execpath`, `npm_node_execpath`, `npm_command`): npm passes a
+project's config on to the processes it starts, and a node-based helper would
+otherwise load whatever a repository's `node-options` names. A helper that
+exits while a background process it started still holds its stdout is read
+for 250 ms more; the background process is not stopped. On Windows a helper
+in a directory whose path is longer than 258 characters is refused, as
+Windows cannot start a program there.
+
+On Windows, `.cmd` and `.bat` helpers are run through `cmd.exe`, in their own
+directory; a path that contains `"` or `%` is refused.
+
+Callers fill this option only from config they trust (npm: the user and
+global files): the helper is executed. It is deliberately not read from the
+config-key namespace: a `//<registry>/:tokenHelper` key among the options does
+nothing, because callers commonly merge every `.npmrc` key they load, a
+project's included, into the options they pass, and those keys have only ever
+been data.
+
+```
+{
+  tokenHelpers: {
+    '//npm.corp.example.com/': '/usr/local/bin/corp-registry-token'
+  }
+}
+```
+
 ##### <a name="opts-userAgent"></a> `opts.userAgent`
 
 * Type: String
